  "script": {
    "segments": [
      {
        "narration": "Six hundred nuclear missiles were meant to hide under Greenland. The plan was called Iceworm: four thousand kilometres of tunnels cut into a glacier.",
        "visual_query": "greenland ice sheet aerial expanse",
        "visual_fallback": "ice sheet"
      },
      {
        "narration": "The cover story was science. Camp Century, 1959, a hundred and fifty miles from Thule Air Base, powered by a portable reactor.",
        "visual_query": "arctic military base snow tunnels",
        "visual_fallback": "snow base"
      },
      {
        "narration": "Denmark approved a research station on its territory. Denmark was never told about the missiles.",
        "visual_query": "greenland glacier blue ice crevasse",
        "visual_fallback": "blue glacier"
      },
      {
        "narration": "Then the ice began closing in. By mid-1962 the reactor room ceiling had sagged so far it had to be jacked up five feet.",
        "visual_query": "ice tunnel snow passage interior",
        "visual_fallback": "ice tunnel"
      },
      {
        "narration": "Ice cores showed the sheet moving faster than engineers assumed. The camp was evacuated in 1965 and closed in 1966, and the waste stayed down there. A 2016 study warns the ice above it could start melting by 2100.",
        "visual_query": "greenland meltwater ice surface",
        "visual_fallback": "melting ice"
      }
    ],
    "segment_count": 5,
